<div id="intro" class="chart-frame" hidden>
	<div class="chart-card chart-card--intro" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="intro-title">
		<header class="chart-head">
			<div>
				<p class="chart-eyebrow">Welcome</p>
				<h2 id="intro-title">When and where do fires burn?</h2>
			</div>
			<button type="button" class="chart-close intro-close" aria-label="Close">
				<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 4l8 8M12 4l-8 8"></path></svg>
			</button>
		</header>
		<p class="intro-lede">NASA satellites have scanned the whole planet for fires every day since 2000. This globe lets you look up the fire history of any place on Earth.</p>
		<ol class="intro-steps">
			<li><span><b>Find a place.</b> Drag to spin the globe, scroll to zoom, then click anywhere.</span></li>
			<li><span><b>Pick a view.</b> Six buttons appear. Each one answers a different question about fires near that spot.</span></li>
			<li><span><b>Look closer.</b> Hover over a chart for exact numbers. Press <kbd>Esc</kbd> or <span class="intro-x">×</span> to go back.</span></li>
		</ol>
		<dl class="intro-terms">
			<div>
				<dt>Fire day</dt>
				<dd>A day when a satellite saw at least one fire in a small patch of land, about 7 miles (11 km) across. Every view counts fire days within 100 miles of where you click, so a busy year can have thousands of them.</dd>
			</div>
			<div>
				<dt>MODIS and VIIRS</dt>
				<dd>The fire-spotting cameras on NASA and NOAA satellites. MODIS has been watching since 2000. VIIRS is sharper and joined in 2012, so it can spot smaller fires.</dd>
			</div>
		</dl>
		<section class="intro-more" aria-labelledby="intro-how">
			<h3 id="intro-how">How the record is made</h3>
			<p>Click the globe, search for a place, or jump to Home. A 100-mile circle is drawn, and that spot’s fire calendar is loaded week by week, from 2000 onward. The numbers are NASA’s daily detections after the cleanup below. Weeks the archive does not cover are left blank instead of counted as zero.</p>
		</section>
		<section class="intro-more" aria-labelledby="intro-clean">
			<h3 id="intro-clean">How the data was sanitized</h3>
			<p>The raw files are one row per satellite detection. Most of each row is thrown out before anything is stored. What remains is a count of fire days per patch, per week:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Only vegetation fires are kept. In the source files that is type 0. Volcanoes, industry, and other hot spots are dropped, along with broken dates and positions off the planet.</li>
				<li>Each kept detection is snapped to a 0.1° patch, about 7 miles (11 km) on a side. A patch counts at most once per UTC day, so many pixels of the same fire become one fire day.</li>
				<li>Those days are totaled into ISO weeks. A rare 53rd week is folded into week 52. The stored row is only the patch, the year, the week, and the number of fire days.</li>
				<li>Confidence, fire brightness, pixel size, and which pass of the satellite saw the fire are not kept.</li>
				<li>From 2012, Suomi-NPP VIIRS is cleaned the same way and added only where MODIS had no fire that day. A day both cameras saw stays one day. NOAA-20 and NOAA-21 are not included. In 2012, VIIRS starts on day 19 of the year.</li>
				<li>The MODIS archive itself starts in November 2000, at week 44. 2026 is not a full year yet, so those weeks stay blank.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>
		<p class="intro-source"><a href="https://github.com/halceon-inc/vulgarcommander" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source on GitHub</a></p>
		<button type="button" class="intro-go">Start exploring</button>
	</div>
</div>
<div id="ring-frame" class="chart-frame" hidden>
